\code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optim()}} from several starting points, so estimates of zero appear as very small
rates. Under this model the tip states hold information on \code{gamma} and \code{mu} only through
their pattern on the tree, so with few species, or with a community holding nearly all or
nearly none of them, the likelihood surface is flat and the estimates are poor. In
\code{data-raw/validation_damocles.R}, ten communities simulated with \code{gamma = mu = 1} on trees of
60 tips gave estimates of \code{mu} from zero to several thousand, with the same likelihoods as
\code{DAMOCLES::DAMOCLES_ML()}; the ratio of the two rates is better estimated than each one.

With \code{shared = TRUE}, \code{fit_damocles()} estimates one pair of rates for all communities by
maximising the sum of their log-likelihoods, which treats the communities as independent
realisations of the same process on the same tree. Pooling communities narrows the
estimates: in \code{data-raw/validation_damocles.R}, with \code{gamma = mu = 1} on trees of 60 tips and
height one (30 fits each), 90\% of the estimates of \code{gamma} fell between 0.49 and 6.0 from one
community and between 0.75 and 1.27 from twenty, and those of \code{mu} between zero and 18.9 from
one and between zero and 1.8 from twenty. \code{mu} stays the harder rate, and some fits still put
it near zero. The
assumption of independence matters: neighbouring sites that share their colonisation history,
or communities nested in one another, are not independent replicates and pooling them
overstates the information on the rates. Twice the difference between the summed
log-likelihoods of separate and shared fits can be compared with a chi-squared distribution
on \eqn{2 (m - 1)}{2 * (m - 1)} degrees of freedom, with \eqn{m} communities, to ask whether
the rates differ among communities.

With \code{sympatry = NULL}, \code{fit_damocles()} also estimates the probability that a present
ancestor leaves both daughters present, on the logit scale. A single community says almost
nothing about it: in \code{data-raw/validation_damocles.R}, with \code{sympatry = 0.5} and
\code{gamma = mu = 1} on trees of 60 tips, 90\% of the estimates fell between 0 and 1 from one
community, between 0.26 and 0.81 from five and between 0.41 and 0.60 from twenty. Estimate it
with shared rates, and compare the fit with that of \code{sympatry = 0} by a likelihood ratio (one
degree of freedom, on the boundary of the parameter space, so the chi-squared p-value is
conservative) to ask whether strictly allopatric speciation is enough.

